  "preview": "Tomas wants to build a giant backyard Jenga set out of 2x4s. The lumber store sells 8-foot boards for $5.47. How many boards and how much will it cost?",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Use multiplication, division, and addition of rational numbers (including fractions and decimals) to solve a multi-step real-world problem; estimate before computing; and reason about leftover material.",
    "studentPrompt": "Tomas wants to build a giant backyard Jenga set for his cousin's birthday party.\n\nA standard Jenga set has 54 blocks. Each block in Tomas's giant version needs to be:\n- 10 1/2 inches long\n- 3 1/2 inches wide\n- 1 3/4 inches thick\n\nTomas is buying 2x4 lumber from the hardware store. (A 2x4 is actually 1 1/2 in by 3 1/2 in — wide and thick are already right.) Each board is 8 feet long and costs $5.47.\n\nACT 1: What questions come to mind? What information do you need? Make an estimate: how many boards do you think he'll need?\n\nACT 2:\n1. How many of the 10 1/2 in blocks can Tomas cut from ONE 8-foot board? (Watch your units.)\n2. How many boards does he need to make all 54 blocks?\n3. How much will the lumber cost (before tax)?\n\nACT 3: Compare to your estimate. How close were you?\n\nEXTENSION: How much wood (in inches) is left over after cutting? Is there enough scrap for him to make a 55th block as a spare?",
    "image": {
      "alt": "A stylized stack of wooden Jenga-style blocks in a backyard scene with grass at the base and a few floating blocks tumbling.",
      "svg": "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 220\" font-family=\"system-ui, sans-serif\"><defs><linearGradient id=\"sky2\" x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"1\"><stop offset=\"0\" stop-color=\"#cfe6f2\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"#e8f4d8\"/></linearGradient><linearGradient id=\"woodB\" x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"1\"><stop offset=\"0\" stop-color=\"#e0b878\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"#b88440\"/></linearGradient></defs><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"480\" height=\"195\" fill=\"url(#sky2)\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"195\" width=\"480\" height=\"25\" fill=\"#7cb35a\"/><g stroke=\"#5e8b40\" stroke-width=\"1\" opacity=\"0.6\"><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"195\"/><line x1=\"50\" y1=\"208\" x2=\"55\" y2=\"196\"/><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"206\" x2=\"95\" y2=\"195\"/><line x1=\"410\" y1=\"207\" x2=\"415\" y2=\"195\"/><line x1=\"450\" y1=\"206\" x2=\"455\" y2=\"195\"/></g><g stroke=\"#7a5a2a\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"><rect x=\"180\" y=\"175\" width=\"120\" height=\"20\" fill=\"url(#woodB)\"/><rect x=\"180\" y=\"155\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" fill=\"url(#woodB)\"/><rect x=\"220\" y=\"155\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" fill=\"url(#woodB)\"/><rect x=\"260\" y=\"155\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" fill=\"url(#woodB)\"/><rect x=\"180\" y=\"135\" width=\"120\" height=\"20\" fill=\"url(#woodB)\"/><rect x=\"180\" y=\"115\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" fill=\"url(#woodB)\"/><rect x=\"220\" y=\"115\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" fill=\"url(#woodB)\"/><rect x=\"260\" y=\"115\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" fill=\"url(#woodB)\"/><rect x=\"180\" y=\"95\" width=\"120\" height=\"20\" fill=\"url(#woodB)\"/><rect x=\"180\" y=\"75\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" fill=\"url(#woodB)\"/><rect x=\"220\" y=\"75\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" fill=\"url(#woodB)\"/><rect x=\"260\" y=\"75\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" fill=\"url(#woodB)\"/><rect x=\"180\" y=\"55\" width=\"120\" height=\"20\" fill=\"url(#woodB)\"/></g><g stroke=\"#7a5a2a\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"><rect x=\"330\" y=\"160\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" fill=\"url(#woodB)\" transform=\"rotate(18 355 167)\"/><rect x=\"380\" y=\"130\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" fill=\"url(#woodB)\" transform=\"rotate(-22 405 137)\"/><rect x=\"95\" y=\"170\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" fill=\"url(#woodB)\" transform=\"rotate(-12 120 177)\"/></g><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"40\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#fff3b0\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><g fill=\"#ffffff\" opacity=\"0.85\"><ellipse cx=\"380\" cy=\"35\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"8\"/><ellipse cx=\"395\" cy=\"30\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"6\"/></g></svg>"
    },
    "launch": "Show a small Jenga set if you have one, or just hold up a single block. Tell Tomas's story. Ask Act 1: 'What do you want to know? What do you need?' Collect questions on the board. Then reveal the dimensions and price, and turn them loose.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Optional: a Jenga block to pass around",
      "Optional: calculator"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "rational-multiplication",
      "rational-division",
      "unit-conversion",
      "estimation",
      "real-world-application"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Excellent BTC task. Three sharp diamonds: (1) the unit conversion 8 ft = 96 in trips up groups who divide 8 / 10.5; (2) the integer division — you can't cut 9.14 blocks from a board, you cut 9 and have scrap; (3) the ceiling division for total boards — 54 / 9 = 6 exactly, but the extension forces them to revisit waste.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: Convert 8 ft to inches (96 in). Then 96 / 10.5 = 9.142... Strong groups round DOWN to 9 blocks per board (you can't cut a partial block).\n- Q2: 54 blocks / 9 blocks-per-board = 6 boards exactly. Some groups divide 54 / 9.14 ≈ 5.9 and round up to 6 — same answer, different reasoning.\n- Q3: 6 × $5.47 = $32.82.\n- Extension: Each board has 96 - (9 × 10.5) = 96 - 94.5 = 1.5 in of scrap. Across 6 boards that's 9 in of scrap. A 55th block needs 10.5 in. Not enough.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Forgetting to convert 8 ft to 96 in (computing 8 / 10.5 = 0.76 'blocks per board' — clearly wrong, but happens).\n- Rounding 9.14 UP to 10 blocks per board (you'd run out of wood).\n- For total cost, multiplying $5.47 by 54 blocks instead of 6 boards.\n- For the extension, summing scrap as a single 9-in piece (the scraps are separate, not joinable).",
    "discussionQuestions": "- How did your group handle the 9.14 from the division? Why round down and not up?\n- One group thought they needed 7 boards 'just in case.' Is that a math answer or a real-world answer?\n- For the extension, the scraps add up to 9 inches — enough length, you'd think. Why can't Tomas make a 55th block?",
    "extensions": "- Tomas's hardware store also sells 10-foot 2x4s for $6.79. Is it cheaper to buy 10-footers or 8-footers for this project? Justify with the cost-per-block.\n- Tomas wants the giant set to be a regulation Jenga ratio: blocks should be 3 times as long as they are wide. His blocks are 10 1/2 in long and 3 1/2 in wide — perfect. What if his cousin's set needs to be SHORTER for smaller hands — blocks 9 in long and 3 in wide? How many of those fit on an 8-foot board, and what's the new cost for 54?\n- The local sales tax is 7%. What's the actual out-the-door price?",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. 8 ft = 96 in. 96 / 10.5 = 9.142... Tomas can cut 9 blocks per board (the 0.142 is scrap).\n2. 54 / 9 = 6 boards.\n3. 6 × $5.47 = $32.82.\nExtension: Per board scrap = 96 - 9(10.5) = 1.5 in. Total scrap across 6 boards = 9 in. A 55th block needs 10.5 in CONTIGUOUS — the 1.5 in scraps can't be joined. So no spare. Tomas would need a 7th board ($5.47 more) for any spares.",
